## GraphQL API Guide

This project was created **without a database**, so it uses an in-memory `MockRepository` (wired in `cmd/main.go`). Data is **not durable** — it resets on restart. Add a database with `gopamin new … -f graphql -d <database>` for persistence.

The GraphQL API is a **single `POST /graphql` endpoint** — there is no GraphiQL in-browser IDE. Send each operation as JSON `{"query": "<operation>"}` (the port is `PORT` in `.env`, default `8080`):

```text
curl --location 'http://localhost:8080/graphql' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json'{{if .Auth}} \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <JWT>'{{end}} \
--data '{"query": "{ users(limit: 20) { items { id name } next_cursor } }"}'
```
{{if .Auth}}
### Authentication

`POST /graphql` requires a JWT **bearer token** — without one you get `401`. Get a token from `POST /login` (credentials `AUTH_USERNAME` / `AUTH_PASSWORD` in `.env`), then send it as `Authorization: Bearer <JWT>` (as shown above):

```text
curl --location 'http://localhost:8080/login' --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--data '{"username": "admin", "password": "change-me"}'
# -> {"token":"<JWT>"}
```
{{end}}
### Operations

- **List users** — cursor-paginated. `users` returns a `UserPage { items, next_cursor }`; pass `limit` (default `20`, max `100`) and a `cursor` from a previous page:
  `{ users(limit: 20, cursor: "") { items { id name } next_cursor } }`
- **Fetch one:** `{ user(id: "<USER_ID>") { id name } }`
- **Create:** `mutation { create(name: "John Doe") }` — returns the new user's id
- **Update:** `mutation { update(id: "<USER_ID>", name: "Jane Doe") }` — returns the affected row count
- **Delete:** `mutation { delete(id: "<USER_ID>") }` — returns the affected row count
{{if .Broker}}
### Events

On a create, the service publishes an event to **{{.Broker}}**, and a consumer loop runs alongside the server. See the "Broker workflow" section of `AGENTS.md`.

**{{.Broker}} runs in a container**: start it with `make docker-up` before `make run`, or the app cannot reach the broker. `make docker-down` stops it (`make docker-down-rm-vol` also drops the volume).
{{end}}{{if .Observability}}
### Observability

`make docker-up` also starts an **OpenTelemetry Collector** (config: `configs/otel-collector.yaml`); the app exports traces and metrics over OTLP — see the `OTEL_*` keys in `.env`.
{{end}}